  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are purely theoretical and conceptual, relying on literature synthesis and qualitative expert interviews rather than empirical modeling or quantitative evaluation of a specific tax implementation.",
      "what": "This study synthesizes agglomeration theory and Land Value Taxation (LVT) through a systematic literature review and expert interviews. It establishes a conceptual framework showing how taxing unimproved land values can internalize spatial externalities and realign incentives within rigid urban environments.",
      "why": "It bridges a gap between urban economics and tax policy, demonstrating theoretically that LVT can simultaneously enhance urban productivity and mitigate wealth disparities by targeting the spatial and temporal domains of agglomeration."
